  title={A Graph Neural Network Framework for Colorectal Liver Metastasis Tumor Burden Evaluation}, 
  year={2026},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={1488-1493},
  abstract={Colorectal cancer starts in the colon or rectum and often develops from small polyps. When it spreads, it most commonly reaches the liver first forming colorectal liver metastases (CRLM) and it greatly affects patient survival. Common risk factors include older age, unhealthy diet, obesity, excess smoking and consumption of excess alcohol. By early prediction it could be treated by surgery and at late prediction it could be treated with a blend of surgery and chemotherapy. Existing models automatically segment tumors which reduces manual work for doctors. Existing models ignore relationships between patients and deeper analysis of tumor texture and tumor burden. This project introduces an Edge-Weighted Graph Neural Network that uses segmented tumor features and links similar patients. The model predicts tumor burden by learning from both individual features and patient relationships, giving more accurate results for clinical use. The experimental evaluation on TCIA CRLM dataset shows that the proposed method achieves an accuracy of 88.46%, outperforming traditional machine learning and unweighted graph models.},
  keywords={Modeling;Tumors;Liver;Metastasis;Training;Graph neural networks;Dies;Printing;Labeling;Lesions;Graph Neural Network;Colorectal Liver Metastasis;Tumor Burden;Radiomics},
