  abstract={Accurate cancer staging plays a central role in guiding clinical management and treatment decisions, yet traditional staging systems based primarily on anatomical assessment often overlook tumor biological heterogeneity. Radiogenomics offers an integrative approach that combines quantitative imaging features with molecular data to enhance cancer characterization. This paper presents an integrative radiogenomic framework for cancer stage prediction, with a focus on ovarian cancer. Radiomic features extracted from Computed Tomography (CT) scans capture tumor morphology and heterogeneity, while gene expression data from TCGA dataset provide complementary molecular insights. These modalities are processed in parallel and integrated into a unified feature space for machine-learning-based stage classification. In addition, this study reviews existing radiomics, genomics, and radiogenomics approaches for cancer staging, highlighting the limited application of multimodal models in ovarian cancer. Key challenges, including limited paired datasets, feature variability, and interpretability, are discussed. The proposed framework aims to support non-invasive, biologically informed cancer staging and contribute to the advancement of precision oncology.},
