  pages={1-7},
  abstract={As breast cancer incidence rates are rising to alarming levels and remain a serious and major contributor to cancer deaths for women worldwide, early detection and accurate diagnosis of breast cancer is essential. Machine learning is also providing tools to assist with diagnosis through its advanced predictive analytics capabilities, but there remain problems with feature selection and model classifications. This study proposes a model for predicting breast cancer diagnosis using stacked ensemble learning. The framework utilizes the Boruta algorithm to first identify the most important predictive features in the breast cancer dataset and then a stacking classifier is built with Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP), and a HistGradientBoosting Classifier as the stack-level meta-model. The proposed model exhibited strong classification performance in distinguishing benign tumors from malignant tumors. The framework achieved classification accuracy at approximately 98% along with strong precision, recall, and F1score outputs. While the proposed method merged Boruta-based feature selection and ensemble classification methods into a framework that exhibited strong classification accuracy, the study has identified opportunities to develop a framework back into an early-stage breast cancer diagnosis process.},
