  number={},
  pages={1-8},
  abstract={Proteomics research for breast cancer classification has advanced rapidly with the incorporation of machine learning (ML). Despite being quite popular among researchers, single-learning algorithms continue to face significant obstacles. In particular, individual ML models (base models) frequently encounter limitations when faced with the high-dimensional nature of proteomic breast cancer data. Proteomic data with thousands or even millions of features impede ML models's capacity to capture their complexity in full. In this study, we propose PhosStack, a novel regularized stacking learning approach that systematically integrates, in a unified ensemble, diverse base-learners (XGB, Random Forest, KNN, OneVsRest), each of which learns complementary patterns in the feature space. In a two-stage structure, a selected meta-learner (logistic regression) optimally weights the base-learners' predictions to minimize generalization error. PhosStack enhances classification performance on a multiclass tabular breast cancer subtype proteomic dataset. PhosStack yielded a consistent improvement $(5-26 \%)$ over single-model approaches and achieved high predictive performance $(95 \%-96 \%)$ in every evaluated performance metric. In addition, at the meta-learner level, the influence of each base-learner in classifying breast cancer subtypes was systematically analyzed and illustrated. Several biomarkers, with previously unexplored links to breast cancer, were also identified. These hold promise for consideration and future use as diagnostic, prognostic, and therapeutic targets. In general, the findings highlight the urgent need for sophisticated ML frameworks in translational breast cancer proteomics by reinforcing the potential of stacking in shaping next-generation diagnostics and advancing the frontier of precision medicine.},
