  pages={1-6},
  abstract={Lung cancer remains one of the major leading causes of cancer death around the world, where early and accurate diagnosis is key to improving patient outcomes. Conventional methods of diagnosing through manual reading of computed tomography (CT) scans are prone to human error and subjectivity. These disadvantages are the impetus for this work, in which a computerized lung cancer classification framework utilizing deep learning feature extraction fusion with machine learning classifier optimization with metaheuristics is proposed. Pretrained ResNet50 is utilized to learn CT image discriminative features. PCA is applied for reducing the dimensionality of the feature space and retaining its most explanatory components as a way of preventing overfitting and reducing computational load. SMOTE is employed to address the problem of class imbalance, which is common in medical imaging. The features are learned and classified with a Support Vector Machine (SVM) as the baseline. For the purpose of improving classification accuracy, SVM hyperparameters are optimized using two metaheuristic algorithms, Jaya Optimization and Grey Wolf Optimization (GWO). Experimental results demonstrate that the optimal SVM models perform better than the baseline SVM with high accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score, thereby establishing the effectiveness of the proposed system in safe lung cancer detection.},
  keywords={Support vector machines;Accuracy;Computed tomography;Metaheuristics;Lung cancer;Feature extraction;Medical diagnostic imaging;Residual neural networks;Principal component analysis;Image classification;Lung cancer;computed tomography;support vector machine;ResNet50;principal component analysis;SMOTE;Jaya optimization;Grey Wolf optimization;deep feature extraction;medical image classification},
