  pages={1-6},
  abstract={Cancer remains a major global health burden, with 19.3 million new cases and 10.3 million deaths in 2023, projected to rise to 28.4 million cases by 2040. While surgery, chemotherapy, and radiotherapy have long been foundational, their limitations—non-specific cytotoxicity, resistance, and reduced quality of life—necessitate more precise interventions. This review integrates three converging domains shaping the future of oncology: digital health technologies, biotechnology innovations, and advanced therapeutic modalities. Literature spanning digital health, multi-omics, structural biology, immunotherapy, photodynamic therapy (PDT), nanotechnology, antibody-drug conjugates (ADCs), CAR-T, and CRISPR was analyzed alongside computational frameworks for drug repurposing and network-based systems biology. Evidence indicates that AI-enabled imaging and EHR analytics reduce diagnostic delays by up to 30 % and improve treatment matching by 20-25%. Immunotherapies such as checkpoint inhibitors achieve durable responses in melanoma and NSCLC but face resistance in “cold” tumors. CAR-T therapies deliver remission rates exceeding 70 % in hematologic cancers, though solid tumors remain challenging. PDT combined with checkpoint blockade enhances immunogenic cell death, while nanotechnology improves therapeutic index by lowering systemic toxicity. ADCs and PARP inhibitors show significant progression-free survival (PFS) benefits in refractory cancers. Computational frameworks—GraphRepur, KG-Predict, and cryo-EM/AI—accelerate identification of repurposable drugs and drug-target interactions, validated in preclinical and early clinical studies. Integrating digital health, biotechnology, and advanced therapeutics enhances precision, durability, and safety of cancer treatments. However, challenges such as therapy resistance, inequitable access, and regulatory harmonization remain.},
  keywords={Drugs;Biotechnology;Technological innovation;Systems biology;Medical treatment;Immunotherapy;Electronic healthcare;Nanotechnology;Cancer;Tumors;Artificial intelligence;Biotechnology;Cancer immunotherapy;Computational drug repurposing;Cryo-electron microscopy;Digital health;Multi-omics integration;Nanotechnology;Photodynamic therapy;Systems biology},
