  title={Pan-Cancer Analysis for the Identification of Common Oncogenic Biomarkers Using Platelet RNA-Sequencing Data}, 
  year={2026},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={100-105},
  abstract={Cancer diagnosis and prognosis increasingly rely on molecular biomarkers that reflect shared oncogenic pathways across tumor types. In this study, a pan-cancer transcriptomic analysis was performed using platelet RNAsequencing data from GEO to identify common oncogenic biomarkers across breast, colorectal, glioblastoma, hepatobiliary, non-small cell lung and pancreatic cancers. Global expression patterns were assessed after batch correction followed by differential expression analysis with edgeR. A total of 407 common DEGs were subjected to pathway enrichment analyses using GSEA, Gene Ontology, Reactome, Hallmark, KEGG and WikiPathways databases. Protein-protein interaction, expression-to-kinase interaction and transcription factor enrichment analyses were conducted to identify regulatory hubs. Breast and lung cancers showed predominantly downregulated genes whereas glioblastoma, pancreatic and colorectal cancers showed upregulated genes while hepatobiliary cancer exhibits a relatively balanced distribution. Enrichment analyses consistently revealed plateletassociated pathways including platelet activation, thrombin signaling, coagulation and calcium-dependent signaling, along with metabolic pathways such as glycolysis and the pentose phosphate pathway. In contrast, ribosomal biogenesis, translation and immune-related pathways were significantly downregulated with the strongest suppression observed in glioblastoma. Reactome pathways showed the highest enrichment significance among all databases. Network analyses suggested that transcription factors such as MAX, CREB1, NFYB, NFYA, TAF1, UBTF, YY1, GABPA and ELF1 may function as central regulatory hubs commonly observed across multiple cancer types. Overall, this study highlights plateletdriven signaling, calcium homeostasis, coagulation and transcriptional regulation as core pan-cancer features supporting the potential of platelet-derived transcriptomic biomarkers for non-invasive cancer diagnosis.},
  keywords={Cancer;Tumors;Printing;Biomarkers;RNA;Proteins;Transcriptomics;Ontologies;Cells (biology);Coagulation;RNA-sequencing;Platelet transcriptome;GEO;Differential gene expression;Transcription factors;Biomarkers},
